{"term":"بيض","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"bayḍ","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":48,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":93,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The most universally recognized colloquial Arabic slang for testicles, literally 'eggs'; moderately crude and understood across all dialect zones.","english_term":"eggs","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Plural of بيضة (egg); distinguished from the clinical البيضتان by the absence of the definite article and dual morphology; widely used in expressions for 'kick in the balls' and metaphorically for courage; recognized by virtually all adult Arabic speakers."}
{"term":"كور","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"kūr","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":66,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":60,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude dialectal slang for testicles emphasizing spherical shape; used as an insult intensifier as well as a literal anatomical reference.","english_term":"balls","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Likely related to the root for 'sphere'; most prevalent in Gulf and Iraqi dialects; used in insults and frustration expressions; less pan-Arabic than بيض or كرات."}
{"term":"خرز","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"kharaz","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":50,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":52,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Colloquial slang for testicles literally meaning 'beads' or 'glass beads'; uses the bead metaphor for the smooth rounded shape of the testes.","english_term":"beads","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Primary meaning is decorative glass or stone beads; the testicle metaphor extends this to smooth rounded objects; less widely recognized as genital slang than بيض or كرات; dialectal distribution strongest in Levantine Arabic."}
{"term":"حجارة","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ḥijāra","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_crude","crude":60,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":72,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Crude slang for testicles meaning 'stones' or 'rocks'; parallels the English 'stones' metaphor, emphasizing hardness, solidity, and weight.","english_term":"stones","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Plural of حجر (stone/rock); the 'stones' metaphor for testicles appears cross-linguistically; also used metaphorically for courage; present across Levantine, Gulf, and North African dialects."}
{"term":"كرات","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"kurāt","category":"organs","subcategory":"testicles","concept":"testicles","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":55,"technical":3,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":87,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Widely-used colloquial slang for testicles literally meaning 'balls' (spheres); directly parallels English 'balls' in meaning, crudeness level, and metaphorical extension to courage.","english_term":"balls","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Plural of كرة (ball/sphere); used in expressions equivalent to 'break my balls' (تكسر كراتي = you're annoying me) and for courage; one of the most widely recognized testicle slang terms across all Arabic dialects."}
{"term":"ثدي","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"thady","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breast","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"clinical","crude":20,"technical":83,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard classical and clinical Arabic term for the breast or mammary gland; used in medical, anatomical, and formal health contexts for the breast in both sexes.","english_term":"breast","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Classical Arabic term documented in the Quran and classical literature primarily in lactation contexts; plural is أثداء (athdāʾ); carries cultural sensitivity in public discourse despite clinical status; widely recognized by all adult speakers."}
{"term":"صدر","lang":"ar","script":"arab","romanization":"ṣadr","category":"organs","subcategory":"breasts","concept":"breast","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"euphemism","crude":8,"technical":58,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The standard Arabic word for 'chest'; in sexual or romantic contexts functions as a socially acceptable euphemism for the female breasts, referencing the anterior thoracic area rather than the mammary glands specifically.","english_term":"chest","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Extremely common word with multiple meanings: chest (body part), front/head of an organization, and emotional bosom; used widely in poetry and colloquial speech as a modesty-preserving alternative to ثدي; suitable for mixed-company speech where ثدي would feel clinical or awkward."}
